WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:02.802
If you're watching this channel,
you're probably into technology.

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:05.071
You might even be thinking about a career
in technology.

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:09.042
Well, today we have a special guest,
Tina Williams, a woman to technology

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:13.113
who wants to share some of her sage
career advice, starting with “skills”.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.383
So in the area of skills,
I think what you can

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:19.252
evaluate is
whether you want to go deep or broad.

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:24.257
So when I mean by deep
is that you may decide to enter technology

00:00:24.257 --> 00:00:27.460
and you have a specific love
of a specific area,

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:31.564
and you want to become the subject matter
expert there and devote your career.

00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:35.135
And I've seen a lot of women
do that successfully and rise up

00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:39.039
and become distinguished
engineers and submit many patents.

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:42.108
It's a really great way to
to have your career.

00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:46.012
For me, I started out as a developer.

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:49.649
I was coding in assembler and PL/I long,
long time ago

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:53.820
and I got to the point where I wanted more
and I wanted to experience new things,

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.222
so I wanted to be more involved
with clients.

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.458
I moved over into consulting.

00:00:58.458 --> 00:01:01.494
I then moved over into business
development

00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:07.033
and then on into marketing and eventually
found my way back to software.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:09.669
And now I am a product manager.

00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:11.504
So I've taken all of those experiences

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:14.407
along the way to apply now
to being a product manager.

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:15.442
Oh, that's a great way

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:19.312
of starting deep
and then using that as a springboard

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:21.648
into new sort of areas of technology.

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:25.652
That brings us to the next one,
which is called “leave”.

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:26.612
What's that about that?

00:01:26.612 --> 00:01:28.288
Well, it's not really about leaving.

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:31.991
It's about thinking about when you get in,
if you're going to be moving

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:35.195
from different area to a different area,
thinking about your end game.

00:01:35.195 --> 00:01:37.030
So I always think about...

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.099
Like the next job is what you're saying, right?

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:44.971
Right. So I'm working my way out of my current
position into what I'm going to do next.

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:47.640
You know, leaving open the door
for me to go do something else.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.545
And that's really gets back to my desire
to be creative and innovative.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:54.547
That's really where I'm drawn to

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:58.618
and starting new initiatives
or transformations.

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:02.589
And so once I have that all in place
and I have everything

00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:05.692
operational, it's time to move on to something else.

00:02:05.692 --> 00:02:07.827
So that's one way you can approach
your career,

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:10.430
is to think about that
as you're going into a new job.

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:15.268
I also think it makes you less controlling
and more collaborative, also

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:17.504
because you're thinking about handing it
over.

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:20.173
In fact,
I would argue that, at least in my career,

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:23.376
I've always said every 2 to 3 years
you want to be thinking about moving on

00:02:23.376 --> 00:02:27.914
to a new role, a new responsibility,
if anything, just to keep you being a more

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:32.152
well-rounded employee with more education,
more knowledge that you can share.

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:35.455
That brings us then to “messy”.

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:36.222
So messy.

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:38.725
That's really to me
about getting your hands dirty.

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:42.862
So as I moved around from different job
to different job,

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:46.799
there was always a new type of technology
that I was working with.

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:50.336
And so in order to become comfortable
and confident in that area,

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:53.506
I first took the time to really understand
the technology.

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:56.843
And so, for instance,
I was a product manager within IBM.

00:02:57.177 --> 00:03:01.548
I was rolling--helping to roll out a new
CMS system across all of our

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:04.384
global organizations.

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:07.820
And so first, before enabling
all of those different teams,

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.023
I took the time to really understand it
myself

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.792
and actually be able to use the platform
myself.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.563
And now today
I'm involved in an area of AI,

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:21.000
and so I'm taking the time now
to really understand that platform,

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:24.304
get my hands
dirty, understand how it works,

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:29.409
and therefore much more confident
when I talk to my colleagues and clients

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:30.777
You are also are a lot more authentic

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:34.847
because you have experience with it,
not just from reading but doing.

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:35.682
That's great.

00:03:35.682 --> 00:03:38.651
So “elevator",
that's a rather obscure title.

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:39.986
What's that about?

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:42.355
So it ties in with the rest.

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:44.591
Whereas
if you want to move around in your career,

00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:47.527
you're not likely to do that
without getting to know people.

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:52.332
So a great way to get to know
people is to talk to them in the elevator,

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:57.937
which isn't intuitive
or always considered normal or proper.

00:03:57.937 --> 00:04:03.109
But for me, I've met lots of people
in the elevator at all different levels.

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:07.180
My peer colleagues
and then also executives.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:11.351
And I find that people are very willing
to talk about themselves

00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:12.752
and what area they're in.

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:15.255
And I've learned so much
about different areas to explore

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:17.390
that I would have never found out
about otherwise.

00:04:17.724 --> 00:04:21.160
In fact, that's how Tina and I met--she
was walking by the studio.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.397
We got to talking about career and I
said, “Why don't you come on and tell us

00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:27.033
more about it?” So I'm really glad
that you did reach out like that.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.770
Well, you get the final word
with the category “more”.

00:04:31.571 --> 00:04:32.705
So this is

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.975
something I just think about for all women

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:38.911
and really for a lot of professionals

00:04:39.245 --> 00:04:42.115
is that it's always good to ask for more.

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:46.286
Like always ask for what you want,
always ask about

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:50.490
new areas, always ask for more money
if that's what you think you deserve.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:53.259
I always ask for new opportunities.

00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:56.729
If you don't ask, then no one knows.

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:58.364
No one knows that you care.

00:04:58.364 --> 00:04:59.499
And they may just...

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:01.100
It's not that they don't

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.637
respect or appreciate you,
but they may not prioritize you.

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:06.939
It reminds me of something
one of my mentors actually said

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:10.710
is that “you are your own best advocate,”
so you need to be one.

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:12.945
Well, you get the final word

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:16.449
offering advice to the ladies out there
who are thinking about technology.

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:18.151
What would you say to them?

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:21.387
I would just say
technology has been a great career for me.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:25.291
I've had opportunities to learn,
which is something I really enjoy.

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:26.359
I've had opportunities

00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:29.329
to fly all over the world
and meet all kinds of wonderful people

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:30.963
through technology.

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:33.299
And it's just been a great career.

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:35.401
Excellent! Well,
thank you very much, Tina.

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:38.371
Hey, before you guys leave,
be sure to hit like and subscribe.

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:41.641
And also if you'd like to see some other
